      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order yet. 30 mg is three times the maximum recommended daily dose of amlodipine, which is 10 mg once daily. Even with normal kidney and liver function, a dose that high carries a serious risk of profound hypotension, reflex tachycardia, and peripheral edema. Overdose can also cause prolonged shock and non-cardiogenic pulmonary edema.\n\nIs 30 mg what you intended, or is it a typo? Here are some options:\n\n- **10 mg by mouth once daily.** This is the standard maximum.\n- **5 mg by mouth once daily.** This is the usual starting dose. Most patients are titrated up to 10 mg.\n- **A different drug or combination.** If you need more blood pressure control than 10 mg of amlodipine gives, you could add a second agent, such as an ARB, an ACE inhibitor, or a thiazide.\n\nIf you do want 30 mg, tell me and include the clinical reason. I'll enter it with a note documenting that it was intentional. Otherwise, tell me the dose you want and I'll enter it."
      }
     ],
